            <div xml:id="d357">

                <pb facs="0487" n="369" sameAs="frus:frus1928v02/pg369"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">125.643/79: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-01-03T18:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 3,
                                1929—6 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 9:20 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>4. Department’s 300, August 31, 7 p.m.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>It has been the Legation’s understanding that the establishment of
                        customary naval honors with the Chinese authorities would be resumed in
                        connection with the ceremonies to be observed on the reoccupation of the
                        Nanking consulate. The consulate was reoccupied on December 15th without any
                        ceremonies whatsoever and the question of such formalities has not been
                        otherwise disposed of Admiral Bristol has now inquired of me as to the
                        attitude which he should adopt in this particular.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>… I should however be glad to ascertain the Department’s view with regard
                        to this matter in order that I may inform Admiral Bristol of the course to
                        be followed.</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
